  {
   "id": "fixed",
   "title": "Fixed output regulator",
   "page": 11,
   "description": "The basic negative rail: a negative unregulated supply into IN, the fixed negative output from OUT, and one polarised capacitor on each side to ground. The output capacitor helps the transient response; the input one is needed when the regulator sits far from the supply's filter capacitor. XX in the part name stands for the output voltage.",
   "parts": [
    {
     "ref": "U1",
     "type": "ic",
     "value": "L79XX"
    },
    {
     "ref": "CI",
     "type": "capacitor",
     "value": "2.2 µF",
     "note": "polarised, positive to ground"
    },
    {
     "ref": "CO",
     "type": "capacitor",
     "value": "1 µF",
     "note": "polarised, positive to ground"
    },
    {
     "ref": "VI",
     "type": "terminal",
     "value": "-VI"
    },
    {
     "ref": "VO",
     "type": "terminal",
     "value": "-VO"
    }
   ],
   "notes": [
    "With large capacitors, a high-current diode such as a 1N4001 from input to output guards the regulator against a momentary short on the input."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "split-15v",
   "title": "Split power supply (± 15 V - 1 A)",
   "page": 11,
   "description": "A pair of regulators making +15 V and -15 V at 1 A from +20 V and -20 V: an L7815 on the positive side and an L7915 on the negative, sharing one ground. Each input has its capacitor to ground (0.33 µF on the positive, 2.2 µF on the negative) and each output its own (0.1 µF and 1 µF). A 1N4001 from ground to each output, cathode towards the more positive side, guards against latch-up.",
