 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Family",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "L79xxC is a series of three-terminal regulators with fixed negative outputs of -5, -8, -12 and -15 V and output current up to 1.5 A. The output voltages match the positive L78xx series, so a pair of them makes a split supply. With enough heat sinking they deliver more than 1.5 A. The same parts, with external components, give adjustable voltages or currents. L7912CV-DG is the -12 V part in TO-220 on the Dual Gauge frame."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Protection",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Built in: thermal overload shutdown, short-circuit current limiting and safe-operating-area protection of the output transistor. Output current and power dissipation are limited inside the part, so neither has an absolute maximum figure."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitors",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "CI is required for stability. Its value is for a solid tantalum part; an aluminium electrolytic must be ten or more times as large. CI is needed when the supply filter is some distance from the regulator. CO improves the transient response. Where the capacitors are large, a high-current diode such as a 1N4001 from input to output protects the regulator against a brief short of the input."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Split supplies",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "In the ±15 V split supply built from an L7815 and an L7915, a 1N4001 from ground to each output guards against latch-up."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Raising the output",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Lifting the GND pin with a divider (R2 from OUT to GND pin, R1 from GND pin to ground) multiplies the output by (R1 + R2) / R2. The current through R2 should be more than three times the quiescent current. A 25 µF capacitor from the GND pin to ground is optional and improves transient response and ripple rejection."
  },
  {
   "topic": "More current",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "An external 2N3055 pass transistor driven from the regulator's input lead takes an L7905 to 4 A at -5 V from -10 V; a BD175 across a 0.2 Ω resistor in the pass transistor's emitter sets a 5 A current limit."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Junction to case: 3 °C/W in D²PAK, 5 °C/W in TO-220 and TO-220FP. Junction to ambient: 62.5 °C/W in D²PAK, 50 °C/W in TO-220, 60 °C/W in TO-220FP. The junction may run from 0 °C to 150 °C; the electrical figures hold from 0 °C to 125 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Packages",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "TO-220 comes on a Dual Gauge frame (the -DG order codes) and a Single Gauge frame; the two are compatible, and the only dimension that differs is F (1.23 to 1.32 mm Dual Gauge, 0.51 to 0.60 mm Single Gauge). D²PAK comes from ST and from the subcontractor Wooseok; both suit the ST recommended footprint."
  }
 ],
